very real number.","step_ids":["step-nonequivalence"],"tool_ids":[]}],"open_questions":[],"proof_steps":[{"citations":[],"depends_on":[],"id":"step-factor","justification":"Difference of squares: x^2 - 4 = (x - 2)(x + 2).","statement":"Rewrite the numerator as (x - 2)(x + 2)."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["step-factor"],"id":"step-cancel","justification":"When x ≠ 2, the factor x - 2 is nonzero and may be cancelled.","statement":"For x ≠ 2, ((x - 2)(x + 2))/(x - 2) = x + 2."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":[],"id":"step-domain","justification":"A real quotient is defined precisely when its denominator is nonzero; here x - 2 ≠ 0 exactly when x ≠ 2.","statement":"The original expression is defined exactly for x ∈ R \\ {2}."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["step-domain","step-cancel"],"id":"step-nonequivalence","justification":"At x = 2 the original quotient has denominator zero and is undefined, while x + 2 is defined and equals 4.","statement":"The equality after cancellation holds only on the original domain, not at every real number."}],"question_status":"answered","tool_requests":[]}
